Installation is now a single step from the repository root:

    pip install .                     # or: pip install -e ".[test,docs]"

pyproject.toml is the single source of truth for *which* packages are needed.
The two requirements files fix the versions: requirements.txt in the repository
root pins the required set, and requirements1.txt here is that same set plus the
htesp[fermisurface] extra, with the reasoning behind the pins written above them.
A test keeps the versions in the two files identical.

Python 3.10 or newer. phonopy is installed separately, because it is a binary
tool rather than a library:

    conda install -c conda-forge phonopy

requirements1.txt is one pinned, known-good environment: the required set plus
the htesp[fermisurface] extra. Use it only when a fresh `pip install .` fails to
resolve. It is not the source of truth -- pyproject.toml is -- and it does not
cover htesp[ml], htesp[test] or htesp[docs].

On a new machine, check the result with `htesp-check`: it reports the
architecture, the memory page size and every dependency, and names the extra to
install for anything missing.
